.TH LOGIND 8
.SH NAME
logind \- login daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth/logind
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Logind
is normally started by
.IR svc (8)
to service requests on the
.B inflogin
TCP/IP port,
to provide a `signing' service (identity authentication) for a network.
Ultimately, the client receives a certificate that can be used to establish its identity
with any host that is willing to honour certificates from the certificate's signer.
.PP
The signer constructs the certificate from the contents
of the signer's key file
.BR /keydb/signerkey ,
typically created by
.IR createsignerkey (8),
and
the ID string and password supplied by the client (which are used to access
.IR keyfs (4)
to check identity).
.PP
The protocol involves an exchange of information between the client and server,
as summarised in
.IR login (6).
The client side of this exchange can be managed by the
.B Login
module; see
.IR security-login (2).
.PP
Client and server communicate over the Secure Socket Layer device
.IR ssl (3).
.SS "Failed attempts"
.I Logind
limits online guessing of passwords
(NIST SP 800-53 AC-7, Common Criteria FIA_AFL.1).
After 10 logins in a row for a name that do not prove its password,
further logins for that name are refused for 60 seconds,
before the password is looked up or any key is exchanged,
with the error
.IP
.B "login refused: too many failed attempts; retry in"
.IR n s
.PP
An attempt is counted when it begins and its count cleared only when
the client shows it has the session's key, which only the password gives;
so a wrong password, a client that hangs up part way, a value refused,
a time out and a
.I logind
killed are all counted, and many connections opened at once for one
name are counted as they start.
A login that works clears the name's count.
A name with no account is counted and refused in the same way and the
same words, so the throttle does not tell which names have accounts.
A count not added to for 15 minutes is forgotten, as is a cooldown once
it ends; at most 4096 counts not at the limit are kept, the oldest
dropped first.
Each lock, and each refusal while locked, is written to the console and,
when the audit log is set up
.RI ( auditfs (4)),
to it as an
.B authlock
event.
.PP
Each connection is served by a
.I logind
of its own, so the counts are kept in
.BR /keydb/loginfail ,
one line per name: the SHA-256 of the name in hexadecimal, the count,
when the last attempt began and when the cooldown ends (0 if not set),
in seconds since the epoch.
It is read and written whole under
.BR /keydb/loginfail.lock ,
made exclusively and removed when closed; a lock more than a minute old
is taken to be left by a crash and removed.
Removing an entry, or the file, ends a cooldown at once.
If the file or its lock cannot be had,
.I logind
refuses the login rather than go without the limit.
.SH FILES
.TF /keydb/loginfail.lock
.TP
.B /keydb/keys
.TP
.B /keydb/signerkey
.TP
.B /keydb/loginfail
Failed-attempt counts.
.TP
.B /keydb/loginfail.lock
Its lock.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/auth/logind.b
